00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:48.520
about Contact Center AI.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.660
Contact Center AI is part
of a larger effort at Google

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.200
called Cloud AI.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.010
In Cloud AI, we categorize our
efforts into three categories.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:04.120
Category number one is our
platform-- our AI platform,

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:08.410
which enables machine learning
engineers and data scientists

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:12.250
to run their machine learning,
training, prediction,

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:16.060
and analytics jobs, either in
the cloud using Cloud Machine

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.660
Learning Engine or in a hybrid
or even on-prem environment

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.610
using Kubeflow.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:26.080
The second category is
our pre-trained machine

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.490
learning models, easily
consumable through APIs

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:35.470
like our image recognition,
object recognition models,

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:40.060
translations model, and
natural language model.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.460
If you bring in your
own labeled data,

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:47.610
you can then customize these
pre-trained APIs for you,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.880
for your use cases using
our AutoML technology.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.190
And I'm sure you have all
seen a bunch of announcements

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.920
around AutoML
yesterday and today.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.200
The third category
is our AI solutions.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:04.180
In the solutions category,
we get these pre-trained APIs

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.310
built new--

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.450
pre-trained models and APIs--

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.530
package them together
in a contained solution

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:15.820
focused on a set of
very specific use cases.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:20.350
And Contact Center AI is one
of those solutions focused

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:24.660
on customer care and
contact center use cases.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.580
But how did we get there?

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.810
We talked to a whole bunch of
customers and a lot of partners

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:33.740
and they told us a
lot of pain points.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:38.680
These pain points boil into
three distinct categories.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.850
Category number one is the
infamous long and deep IVR

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:43.970
tree.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:45.810
Press one to go left.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.250
Press two to go sharp right.

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.302
Press three to go
slightly right.

00:03:50.302 --> 00:03:51.760
And I bet you, none
of you actually

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:52.752
enjoys that experience.

00:03:52.752 --> 00:03:54.460
If you do, please do
not raise your hand,

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.190
but anyone that I've talked to
doesn't enjoy that experience.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:00.730
But once you actually
push two about seven times

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.070
and three about six
times and along the way

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.950
you provide your account
number a couple times

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.650
and you finally get
to a live agent,

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.630
that agent turns around
and promptly asks

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:13.460
you, what is your
account number?

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.779
This information that you
provide the IVR at times gets

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:22.810
missed when the IVR transitions
the call to a live agent.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.630
Now that you have provided your
account number for the third--

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:27.990
or n-th time for that matter--

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.670
the agent promptly
puts you on hold,

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:35.470
lets you listen to some music
while the agent on the side

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:40.600
is frantically searching for
the answer to your question.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.960
This makes it for a very
poor caller experience.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:47.560
I don't think any of us
enjoys that experience.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.680
But it also makes it very,
very expensive to operate

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.080
the contact center.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.690
When we talk to our
customers, to our partners,

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:58.060
we figured out that at
some points in time,

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:02.470
it takes up to about $15 to
answer a call that actually

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:03.760
comes to a contact center.

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.750
That's a lot of
money for one call.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:10.690
Also, the agents
are being pushed.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:13.120
They need to find
these answers quickly.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.220
Their desktop is a mess.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:16.320
They have CRM.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:17.440
They have Contact Center.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.000
They have Search.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.830
That results into roughly,
about one out of three agents

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:27.280
churning within 12 months
of joining a contact center.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.500
Makes it very, very expensive
to operate a contact center.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:33.120
So this is why we thought,
how can we actually utilize

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.110
AI to make this experience
better, both for the caller

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.180
and for the agent?

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.870
And that's why we built
Contact Center AI.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:48.420
Contact Center AI has three
major components in it.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.610
The first one is virtual agent.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.130
It is based on our Dialogflow
Enterprise product.

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:59.400
In addition to automating
the common transactional

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.790
and informational
calls, Dialogflow

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.640
allows you to
flatten the IVR tree.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:09.720
It allows you to replace press
one for x, press two for y

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.200
with hello, I'm an
automated agent.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.700
How can I help you today?

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.290
And it can continue
that conversation

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.990
with the caller in a natural
language kind of a way.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:23.370
It also has the ability
to raise the call directly

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.590
to a human agent.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.050
And it will make sure that
all the context gathered

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:32.840
during the call is actually
passed from the automated agent

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:36.580
to the live agent, which
results of the second bucket--

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.970
Agent Assist.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:40.920
We built Agent
Assist to make sure

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:43.080
that the experience
for the live agents

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.590
gets better and
more streamlined.

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:48.790
Agent Assist-- as
its name implies--

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:52.470
will assist the agent
by monitoring the call,

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:56.910
building the context around that
conversation between that agent

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.450
and that customer.

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:02.280
Will go to the knowledge
base, fetches the right answer

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.920
or the right article
that has the answer in it

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.140
and surfaces it to the agent.

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:08.940
So hopefully, you
will never again

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:12.750
be put on hold by
an agent, hopefully.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:15.840
The third piece is
conversational topic modeling.

00:07:18.345 --> 00:07:20.100
Conversational
topic modeling would

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:25.680
allow you to discover topics in
your chat logs and audio logs.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:27.910
Not only will it tell you
what topics your customers

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:29.970
were calling you
about, it will tell you

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:32.910
which key words they used
to articulate that topic.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.470
What were the top sentences they
used to talk about that topic?

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.220
I'm not going to talk
about topic modeling

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.370
that much right now because
you will hear a lot about it

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:43.620
from our partners.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:46.110
By the way, whatever
I've mentioned so far

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:51.990
and I used the term caller, it
works both for audio and phone,

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:55.620
as well as text and chat.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:59.790
We will manage the session
between the contact center

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:04.890
deployment and all the
AIs behind the scenes.

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:06.970
Everything is backed
by a knowledge base.

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:09.120
You can create multiple
knowledge bases

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.020
to back your Agent Assist,
as well as virtual agent.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:19.050
By the way, in this color-coded
slide, Google builds the red.

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:21.215
Partner or the customer
builds the green.

00:08:21.215 --> 00:08:22.890
That's what the colors mean.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.390
I'm not going to
