00:20:45.855 --> 00:20:47.480
JOSHUA HASLETT: I
want to go to Europe.

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:54.600
MARISOL: Do you know where
you would like to go?

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:56.766
JOSHUA HASLETT: No, but I'd
like to talk to somebody

00:20:56.766 --> 00:20:59.600
that can give me some ideas.

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:00.590
MARISOL: No problem.

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:02.510
We have expert
advisors that can help

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:04.190
you find the perfect location.

00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:06.500
Are you interested
in a guided tour?

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:09.230
JOSHUA HASLETT: No, thanks.

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:11.290
MARISOL: OK, we would
be delighted to help

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:12.820
you plan your vacation.

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:16.030
Please hold on while I pass you
to one of our expert advisors

00:21:16.030 --> 00:21:17.870
who can help you plan
a fantastic trip.

00:21:25.725 --> 00:21:27.100
JOSHUA HASLETT:
So at this point,

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:30.610
the call comes through to the
right agent that has expertise

00:21:30.610 --> 00:21:32.830
on European vacation traveling.

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:34.690
And that agent is
handed a transcript

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:38.800
of the voice interaction
between the bot and the human.

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:41.350
And as we start getting
into this conversation,

00:21:41.350 --> 00:21:43.415
we can actually expand
to provide suggestions

00:21:43.415 --> 00:21:44.790
the same way that
we did in chat.

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:58.040
So Acendas vacations is excited
about this opportunity--

00:21:58.040 --> 00:22:01.124
if we can go back to the
presentation monitor.

00:22:01.124 --> 00:22:04.574
Thank you.

00:22:04.574 --> 00:22:05.990
They're really
excited about being

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:09.324
able to focus on improving
matching accuracy for customers

00:22:09.324 --> 00:22:11.240
as they come to their
website and want to find

00:22:11.240 --> 00:22:13.250
that consultative vacation.

00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:15.590
They're also wanting
to increase the success

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:19.540
rate of first-time bookings
and repeat bookings.

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:23.860
Come see more down at our
booth in Moscone West, 1323.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:26.610
Thank you, and at point,
I'll turn it over to Ofer.

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:29.060
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:35.920 --> 00:22:36.910
OFER RONEN: Hello.

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:41.440
How many people here, if they
could do a project in weeks,

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:43.580
would prefer that over months?

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:46.270
Just a show of hands.

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:50.230
And the rest of you maybe
didn't hear the question.

00:22:50.230 --> 00:22:52.990
So Chatbase can
help with just that.

00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:56.470
Teams are building
virtual agents

00:22:56.470 --> 00:23:00.920
for contact centers can
now save a ton of time.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:05.230
So we've heard about how
topic modeling can give you,

00:23:05.230 --> 00:23:09.640
at a high level, what calls
and live chats are about.

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:12.310
Well, what we've
done is we've gone

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:14.980
and at a very specific
use case level,

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:18.580
are able to map out the
flows of those conversations.

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:24.640
All that is made possible by
leveraging Google's machine

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:26.890
learning that's been
developed over many years.

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:28.600
We're within Google.

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:33.850
And the same machine learning
also powers Google Search--

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:38.060
parts of Google Search
and Assistant and Gmail.

00:23:38.060 --> 00:23:40.910
So I'm excited to
say that today, we're

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:43.220
announcing our
early access program

00:23:43.220 --> 00:23:49.740
called Chatbase Enterprise
Edition, which does just that.

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:52.940
And if you step back--

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:57.800
for the last couple years,
we've been doing optimization

00:23:57.800 --> 00:24:01.550
of virtual agents and we've done
that for hundreds of thousands

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:05.750
of virtual agents, processing
billions of messages

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:07.670
to virtual agents.

00:24:07.670 --> 00:24:09.680
And the new part that
we're announcing today

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:16.060
is to help you with insights for
building these virtual agents.

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:18.430
So normally, when a
team wants to build

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:21.250
a virtual agent for
a contact center,

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:25.060
they get experts from within
the company into a room.

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:28.040
Sometimes, they
bring in consultants.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:32.090
And they brainstorm,
what should we build?

00:24:32.090 --> 00:24:35.000
They might brainstorm the flows
and they might think about,

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:36.990
how might people phrase this?

00:24:36.990 --> 00:24:42.380
And they might come up with
maybe, 10, 20, 50 variations.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:46.580
But if you use data, we've
seen that you can get thousands

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:49.170
of variations and
that makes your bots,

00:24:49.170 --> 00:24:51.650
your virtual agents smart.

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:54.950
So really, there's
three shortcomings

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:57.210
to this brainstorm approach.

00:24:57.210 --> 00:25:00.560
One is that you don't
predict all the nuances

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:03.770
of the conversation, all the
edge cases where conversation

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:05.120
might go.

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:12.350
Two is that you miss trends
in the contact center,

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:15.530
whether it's seasonality,
new products,

00:25:15.530 --> 00:25:17.490
or other things like that.

00:25:17.490 --> 00:25:20.030
It just is hard to
keep up with that.

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:25.490
And three-- third shortcoming is
that it's a slow process, very

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:29.940
slow to flesh out everything.

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:31.730
But really what
it's all about is

00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:34.230
it's a black box what goes
on in the contact center.

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:39.330
What are people calling
about, how do they phrase

00:25:39.330 --> 00:25:42.870
the question is
not always clear.

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:45.346
Sometimes, they even
say they don't even

00:25:45.346 --> 00:25:46.470
know what their problem is.

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:49.650
They're trying to figure it out.

00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:55.190
Whether good responses by
agents is also not always clear.

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:56.990
And so this is where
Chatbase comes in.

00:25:56.990 --> 00:26:00.776
So we tag all the parts
of the conversation--

00:26:00.776 --> 00:26:02.150
actually at the
highest level, we

00:26:02.150 --> 00:26:03.566
tag what the
conversation's about.

00:26:03.566 --> 00:26:06.000
This is a billing example.

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:09.230
Someone's calling to
change their bill due date.

00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:11.490
And so we call it the driver.

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:13.010
The highest level is billing.

00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:17.520
The intent level is change my
bill due date-- more specific.

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:19.790
And then we go beyond
that, you see the steps

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:25.130
in that conversation to what
date they want to change it to

00:26:25.130 --> 00:26:29.030
and then, the customer
provides the new date.

00:26:29.030 --> 00:26:31.280
So for each of these
nodes, we extract

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:34.770
thousands of variations.

00:26:34.770 --> 00:26:36.620
For example, this is real data.

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:38.180
When someone
provides a new date,

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:40.650
you expect them to
just give a date.

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:42.880
But we've seen in the
data people say, how

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:45.710
about between the
3rd and the 5th?

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:49.850
Or mid-month on a Friday--

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:51.480
these are real examples.

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:53.620
Most virtual
agents, were like, I

00:26:53.620 --> 00:26:55.900
don't know what
you want from me.

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:57.500
I wasn't trained for this.

00:26:57.500 --> 00:27:00.830
But if you use the
data-driven approach,

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:03.365
it can really handle
all these cases.

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:08.470
So I'm going to talk
about the three steps

00:27:08.470 --> 00:27:11.650
for building data-driven
virtual agents.

00:27:11.650 --> 00:27:17.050
It starts with discovering
what's in your data.

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:20.290
And then you deploy
it to production.

00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:24.720
And then in production, you
optimize the experiences.

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:28.650
And we're going to go
into each one of these.

00:27:28.650 --> 00:27:31.780
So first step of
the discovery phase

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:35.530
is to find what are the
high-level drivers that people

00:27:35.530 --> 00:27:37.150
are calling in about?

00:27:37.150 --> 00:27:39.940
So we use unsupervised
machine learning, also known

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:41.729
as clustering, to
give you a sense

00:27:41.729 --> 00:27:42.895
for what are the key themes.

00:27:46.330 --> 00:27:48.930
And you can see here that
billing, and accounts,

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:52.710
and returns are major
drivers, but that tech support

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:56.210
and payment are not as common.

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.720
So let's go for a second
into the billing and flesh

00:27:58.720 --> 00:28:02.340
that out because it
seems to be common.

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:05.710
So within billing, you see
we've clustered requests

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:09.970
for changing bill due date and
that's a pretty big cluster.

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:13.990
And then, requests for
charges on your bill

00:28:13.990 --> 00:28:17.800
or discounts to your
bill are less common.

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:20.170
At this stage, we use some
supervised machine learning

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:22.570
to clean up the
grouping, but it's really

00:28:22.570 --> 00:28:24.940
the unsupervised that gives
you a sense of where to look

00:28:24.940 --> 00:28:27.520
and what's important.

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:31.480
Next step is to go deeper
into change bill due date

00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:37.020
because it seems to be a large
intent within a large driver.

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:40.830
And so the next step
is to generate--

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:43.140
and this can be done
in a couple hours,

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:45.700
something we've heard takes
a week to four weeks--

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:48.360
you can generate
a detailed flow.

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:52.820
Once that intent is triggered--
change bill due date--

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:57.730
you can see all the ways
those conversations go.

00:28:57.730 --> 00:29:01.350
So in this case,
a common flow is

00:29:01.350 --> 00:29:04.230
to verify the name,
then what date,

00:29:04.230 --> 00:29:06.300
and confirming the new date.

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:08.160
Each one of these
nodes, behind it

00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:12.460
has the thousands of
ways that it was said.

00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:14.340
And Google's one of
the few companies

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:15.960
that understand
language-- it's been

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:18.970
our business for a long time.

00:29:18.970 --> 00:29:24.730
And so it's a good technology
to apply to this problem.

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:31.570
So now we have the building
blocks for the virtual agent

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:32.980
and we've looked at it.

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:36.910
We verified that that it looks
good and it's time to deploy.

00:29:36.910 --> 00:29:38.810
And so you deploy it
to your virtual agent

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:44.830
so that's out there in the
wild being used by customers.

00:29:44.830 --> 00:29:48.375
And then, you want to optimize.

00:29:48.375 --> 00:29:49.750
There's always
going to be things

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:51.940
that you miss, different
things that didn't show up

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:54.190
in the original
data and so this is

00:29:54.190 --> 00:29:57.190
where you have a chance
to use the Chatbase not

00:29:57.190 --> 00:29:58.640
handled report.

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:01.370
It shows you all the cases
where the bot was like,

00:30:01.370 --> 00:30:04.130
I don't know what the
customer is saying.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:06.937
And here we, again,
use machine learning.

00:30:06.937 --> 00:30:08.770
So in the case of, let's
say, my bill timing

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:11.470
won't work and
get paid on second

00:30:11.470 --> 00:30:13.840
so we need a new due date--

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:15.120
those weren't caught.

00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:16.730
But with supervised
machine learning,

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:18.340
we suggest the intent.

00:30:18.340 --> 00:30:21.920
So we tell you this is what
we believe this should map to.

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:25.420
And with human review, you
can accept the whole thing.

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:27.220
If there's one that
you're not sure about,

00:30:27.220 --> 00:30:28.719
you can bring up
the full transcript

00:30:28.719 --> 00:30:34.930
to confirm the context of it
to make sure it's appropriate.

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:36.610
After you've gone
through this step,

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:40.420
the final step is
to check the funnel.

00:30:40.420 --> 00:30:44.590
Are people getting through this
change bill due date funnel?

00:30:44.590 --> 00:30:47.510
For a specific intent,
check the funnel.

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:50.110
You can see there's a drop off,
not everyone gets through it.

00:30:50.110 --> 00:30:53.080
And here, again, you can
bring up sample transcripts

00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:55.450
to see, why are
people dropping off?

00:30:55.450 --> 00:30:58.240
And this is it could be a
customer experience issue

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:02.620
and that will come up
in the transcripts.

00:31:02.620 --> 00:31:06.450
So one large enterprise that
we've done this analysis for

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:10.140
shared that, this way of
building virtual agents

00:31:10.140 --> 00:31:12.570
is like moving at
200 miles per hour,

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:14.250
compared to their
existing way which

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:16.540
is 10 to 20 miles per hour.

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:20.040
So it's like going from
a horse and carriage

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:21.930
to a well-tuned race car.

00:31:25.450 --> 00:31:29.470
Anyone that's interested
in learning more about this

00:31:29.470 --> 00:31:34.360
can visit Chatbase
and request a demo.

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:38.890
You can also come by the Contact
Center AI booth, where I'll be.

00:31:38.890 --> 00:31:40.020
Thank you.

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:41.250
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:45.915
Next, Traci will
come [INAUDIBLE]..

00:31:45.915 --> 00:31:47.710
TRACI GUSHER: Thanks, Ofer.

00:31:47.710 --> 00:31:52.290
So as Ofer said,
contact centers are

00:31:52.290 --> 00:31:55.470
a bit of a black box
and the environment

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:57.510
that we're in today
that's just not acceptable

00:31:57.510 --> 00:32:00.900
because our call centers have
regulatory pressures on them.

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:02.820
They cost us a ton of money.

00:32:02.820 --> 00:32:05.070
And ultimately, they're the
channel to our customers.

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:07.320
And the reputational
risk that we

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:11.520
have if we're not doing
it right is just too big

00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:13.780
not to be doing
something about it.

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:15.870
So KPMG has been working
with a lot of customers

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:18.390
to help them with their
contacts centers and the broader

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:20.700
customer experience process.

00:32:20.700 --> 00:32:23.340
And while we've been doing
a lot of topic modeling,

00:32:23.340 --> 00:32:25.200
we've been doing
it more by our data

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:27.870
scientists and our
engineers using more

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:31.730
customized, tailored approaches.

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:33.830
So when Google gave
us the opportunity

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:38.100
to test this new
topic modeling API,

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:41.510
we were thrilled because
we saw all the opportunity

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:45.920
in the world to take it to our
clients as a high accelerator

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:47.690
to the work that
we've been doing

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:51.200
and bring a great amount of
acceleration and efficiency

00:32:51.200 --> 00:32:53.970
to some of the projects that
we could have done before

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:57.050
and that we will
do in the future.

00:32:57.050 --> 00:32:59.120
Topic modeling really is--

00:32:59.120 --> 00:33:01.160
as we look at it--

00:33:01.160 --> 00:33:04.149
is the foundation of all
of the art of the possible

00:33:04.149 --> 00:33:05.690
that you can do in
the contact center

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:08.390
with artificial intelligence.

00:33:08.390 --> 00:33:10.010
And one of our lead
data scientists--

00:33:10.010 --> 00:33:13.250
Dr. Arthur Franke--
compares data to farming.

00:33:13.250 --> 00:33:17.360
So instead of data as the new
oil, data is the new soil.

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:20.660
And topic modeling, you can
think of as a key seed that

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:22.910
will help you grow
your AI, as you

00:33:22.910 --> 00:33:26.940
move into more AI-enabled
contact centers.

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:30.800
So if we take a look at a more
end-to-end type of approach

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:33.950
and one that starts
with topic modeling

00:33:33.950 --> 00:33:38.060
as its foundational element,
we look at topic modeling

00:33:38.060 --> 00:33:41.420
to drive out the topics that
are telling us what is happening

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:43.070
in these contact centers.

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:45.170
What are people calling about?

00:33:45.170 --> 00:33:46.320
What are the trends?

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:47.240
What are the spikes?

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:48.724
What is the seasonality?

00:33:48.724 --> 00:33:50.390
And then we're applying
insight to them.

00:33:50.390 --> 00:33:52.880
Because once you apply
business insight--

00:33:52.880 --> 00:33:56.240
operational insight over
what's happening in the topic

00:33:56.240 --> 00:33:58.250
modeling, you start
to learn more and more

00:33:58.250 --> 00:34:00.230
not just about your
contact centers,

00:34:00.230 --> 00:34:02.170
but about your
overall organization.

00:34:02.170 --> 00:34:06.320
And those things drive
a lot in terms of value.

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:08.719
We divided into
these two buckets--

00:34:08.719 --> 00:34:11.090
let's improve agents
and let's improve

00:34:11.090 --> 00:34:13.280
our actual optimization.

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:17.389
The optimization that happens
from within the organization,

00:34:17.389 --> 00:34:20.690
the impacts that come from
using artificial intelligence

00:34:20.690 --> 00:34:23.100
in your contact centers.

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:26.670
So let's take a look quickly,
at what we're getting

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:29.460
from the topic modeling itself.

00:34:29.460 --> 00:34:31.770
So at its root, we're
getting increased insights.

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:35.699
We're understanding the intents
and the compound intents

00:34:35.699 --> 00:34:39.030
that occur in these calls or
in these chat logs in order

00:34:39.030 --> 00:34:41.070
for us to really
understand, what

00:34:41.070 --> 00:34:44.010
are the problems our
customers are experiencing?

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:46.980
Are we able to address
those problems?

00:34:46.980 --> 00:34:51.580
And when we address those
problems, are they satisfied?

00:34:51.580 --> 00:34:54.850
It also enables us to take
that business oversight,

00:34:54.850 --> 00:34:58.580
overlay it, and understand
trends in the information.

00:34:58.580 --> 00:35:00.400
So where are my spikes?

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:01.270
Where are my issues?

00:35:01.270 --> 00:35:02.170
Were are my problems?

00:35:02.170 --> 00:35:05.130
Where's abnormality--
there we go--

00:35:05.130 --> 00:35:09.580
where's abnormality in what's
happening in my call centers?

00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:11.620
And can I correct for it?

00:35:11.620 --> 00:35:14.020
And on the benefit
side, there's really

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:17.530
a number of different things
that can drive out of this.

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:21.190
So one of the areas
is eradication.

00:35:21.190 --> 00:35:24.280
There's nothing better
to improve a call center

00:35:24.280 --> 00:35:27.820
than to eradicate the
conversation altogether.

00:35:27.820 --> 00:35:31.510
If you understand that
your customers are having

00:35:31.510 --> 00:35:34.210
a specific problem
that could be solved

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:38.590
by updating your website, by
pushing alerts or notifications

00:35:38.590 --> 00:35:41.410
to them, by making it more
obvious or improved how they

00:35:41.410 --> 00:35:45.340
can answer that question using
one of your mobile channels

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:49.000
and avoid the call or the chat
into your customer contact

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:51.560
center altogether--

00:35:51.560 --> 00:35:52.790
that's the best outcome.

00:35:52.790 --> 00:35:54.400
Your customer never
had a problem--

00:35:54.400 --> 00:35:56.230
that's good customer
experience--

00:35:56.230 --> 00:35:57.910
and the cost doesn't
exist anymore

00:35:57.910 --> 00:36:00.220
because it's
already been solved.

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:01.610
And there's others, as well.

00:36:01.610 --> 00:36:03.520
So you know, feeding
into agent training--

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:04.930
like Ofer talked
about-- in terms

00:36:04.930 --> 00:36:08.200
of being able to build more
virtually-driven agents

00:36:08.200 --> 00:36:11.530
and use the data to
enhance it, is imperative,

00:36:11.530 --> 00:36:14.670
as well as a number
of other things.

00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:17.630
So as I mentioned,
we were really

00:36:17.630 --> 00:36:19.670
thrilled to be given
the opportunity

00:36:19.670 --> 00:36:26.040
to get our hands dirty using
the topic modeling API.

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:31.100
And what we did was
we took 90,000 chat

00:36:31.100 --> 00:36:37.250
logs from our own IT chat log,
from our own IT help desk.

00:36:37.250 --> 00:36:40.760
So the data itself was from
the beginning of this year

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:41.840
through about June.

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:46.640
It had 90,000 chat
logs in it that

00:36:46.640 --> 00:36:53.150
involved about 67 agents and
about 23,000 unique users.

00:36:53.150 --> 00:36:56.660
So it was a pretty
significant sized data set.

00:36:56.660 --> 00:36:59.540
And out of that data set, when
we ran the topic modeling API

00:36:59.540 --> 00:37:02.510
over it, we came out with
about 100 core topics

00:37:02.510 --> 00:37:06.110
that emerged from those chat
log conversations that we then,

00:37:06.110 --> 00:37:09.410
clustered down to about 50-ish.

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:12.530
And as we did this, we
began analyzing the output.

00:37:12.530 --> 00:37:14.460
What can we learn from this?

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:17.000
And here's two examples
of what we found.

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:19.190
First is we looked at a
month over month analysis

00:37:19.190 --> 00:37:21.890
and when we did this, we
found some interesting facts

00:37:21.890 --> 00:37:27.050
that our own help desk didn't
really have readily identified

00:37:27.050 --> 00:37:31.220
as specific issues that
potentially could be resolved

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:34.460
either A, by improving a process
and therefore, eradicating

00:37:34.460 --> 00:37:35.600
the need altogether.

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:38.630
Or using some more
advanced methods,

00:37:38.630 --> 00:37:40.850
like being able to
apply Dialogflow

00:37:40.850 --> 00:37:44.820
to use some more automated
approaches to responses.

00:37:44.820 --> 00:37:49.130
The first was there was a
spike in people requesting

00:37:49.130 --> 00:37:53.500
RSA token password resets.

00:37:53.500 --> 00:37:58.420
And the spike happened right
around audit busy season.

00:37:58.420 --> 00:38:02.150
Well, that's because the
auditors are in the office,

00:38:02.150 --> 00:38:03.860
until busy season hits.

00:38:03.860 --> 00:38:07.070
And then once busy season hits,
they're out at client sites.

00:38:07.070 --> 00:38:09.710
So they haven't used their
RSA token in a month or two,

00:38:09.710 --> 00:38:10.925
they forget their password.

00:38:10.925 --> 00:38:13.550
They have to get it reset before
they go back out to the client

00:38:13.550 --> 00:38:17.032
sites so that they can
VPN in to the network.

00:38:17.032 --> 00:38:19.270
Well for us, this
is an opportunity

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:22.340
to automatically do
pre-notifications.

00:38:22.340 --> 00:38:24.100
Let's look at all
of our auditors

00:38:24.100 --> 00:38:28.420
that haven't used their VPN
in the past four weeks or six

00:38:28.420 --> 00:38:30.370
weeks and let's push
them a note with a link

00:38:30.370 --> 00:38:33.290
that says, if you need to reset
your password, do it here.

00:38:33.290 --> 00:38:35.927
Eradicate the calls altogether.

00:38:35.927 --> 00:38:38.260
Another interesting piece of
analysis that we did was we

00:38:38.260 --> 00:38:44.590
looked at the median agent
chat word length compared

00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:49.120
to the length of the chat, as
well as the number of turns

00:38:49.120 --> 00:38:52.250
in each chat-- so how much back
and forth was occurring in each

00:38:52.250 --> 00:38:53.500
chart--

00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:56.650
along with the total
volume of the chats

00:38:56.650 --> 00:38:59.145
that had occurred in
each one of the topics.

00:38:59.145 --> 00:39:01.580
And we found a couple
of interesting things.

00:39:01.580 --> 00:39:04.570
So one of them was
that if you look

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:07.600
at software installation
as a topic area

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:10.750
and compare it against
conference call information,

00:39:10.750 --> 00:39:12.700
you'll see that
answering these chats

00:39:12.700 --> 00:39:15.340
took about the same
amount of words.

00:39:15.340 --> 00:39:17.710
But look at how
much longer it takes

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:19.720
for the software
installation questions

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:21.820
to be answered properly.

00:39:21.820 --> 00:39:25.630
And the difference there is
for the software installation

00:39:25.630 --> 00:39:28.490
topics, the agent had to
go and do some research.

00:39:28.490 --> 00:39:30.160
They had to go and
get some information.

00:39:30.160 --> 00:39:32.820
They had to go and get the right
type of response to give them.

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:34.060
There were no templates.

00:39:34.060 --> 00:39:36.400
There was no ready
information available for them

00:39:36.400 --> 00:39:39.460
to give to the customers.

00:39:39.460 --> 00:39:44.340
When we look at conference
call, it was copy, paste, done.

00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:49.300
So same amount of words, but
a lot of increased length.

00:39:49.300 --> 00:39:53.080
So conference calls, that
is an immediate low hanging

00:39:53.080 --> 00:39:57.509
fruit ready for the application
of Dialogflow to get automated.

00:39:57.509 --> 00:39:59.800
I don't need to be answering
that with a human anymore.

00:39:59.800 --> 00:40:00.760
It's already templated.

00:40:00.760 --> 00:40:02.350
It's ready to go.

00:40:02.350 --> 00:40:04.400
On the software
installation side,

00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:06.020
I'm looking at this
saying, well, gee,

00:40:06.020 --> 00:40:08.290
if I had an Agent
Assist that was saying

00:40:08.290 --> 00:40:12.820
I heard them ask about
their time and expense,

00:40:12.820 --> 00:40:15.490
here's the information they
need to upgrade their time

00:40:15.490 --> 00:40:17.340
and expense.

00:40:17.340 --> 00:40:20.712
So opportunities for
improvement were apparent

00:40:20.712 --> 00:40:22.170
right from the very
beginning, even

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:26.370
by just doing some
very basic analysis.

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:27.900
But where do we go from here?

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:31.340
So in moving this
forward, the real power

00:40:31.340 --> 00:40:33.270
of topic modeling--
as I mentioned--

00:40:33.270 --> 00:40:35.210
is that topic modeling
is that foundation.

00:40:35.210 --> 00:40:37.530
It's that seed that we can
grow into something else.

00:40:37.530 --> 00:40:41.990
And by overlaying a lot of
the application of analytics

00:40:41.990 --> 00:40:43.970
over this, we're
able to actually do

00:40:43.970 --> 00:40:48.500
some real steady state run
operations in improving the way

00:40:48.500 --> 00:40:51.210
that we're serving our
internal customers.

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:54.690
So one of those ways is turning
this into real monitoring.

00:40:54.690 --> 00:40:58.760
So if I have my topic modeling
occurring over my customer

00:40:58.760 --> 00:41:01.160
channels on an
ongoing basis, then I

00:41:01.160 --> 00:41:03.556
can monitor what's spiking.

00:41:03.556 --> 00:41:05.180
What are the issues
that are coming up?

00:41:05.180 --> 00:41:08.060
What are the issues that I put
a process improvement in place

00:41:08.060 --> 00:41:09.830
for because it was a spike?

00:41:09.830 --> 00:41:12.710
And can I monitor that
it's being eradicated,

00:41:12.710 --> 00:41:14.900
that it's dropping off?

00:41:14.900 --> 00:41:19.760
And one of the other areas
is in active programs.

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:22.470
So we all have a lot of
change in our organizations.

00:41:22.470 --> 00:41:24.470
We're getting a new tool,
or a new technology,

00:41:24.470 --> 00:41:27.020
or a new upgrade, or are
migrating to an operating

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:29.790
system all the time.

00:41:29.790 --> 00:41:33.260
And these programs spike the
need for customer service.

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:36.560
The need for the addressment
of questions and issues.

00:41:36.560 --> 00:41:39.230
And one of those areas
that we recently had was we

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:41.600
implemented a new T&amp;E system.

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:45.110
And we knew that there was going
to be a spike of information,

00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:48.050
a spike of need
for call answering

00:41:48.050 --> 00:41:51.060
when we were implementing
this new system.

00:41:51.060 --> 00:41:53.030
But what they
didn't readily know

00:41:53.030 --> 00:41:55.820
was, what were the very
specific issues people

00:41:55.820 --> 00:41:57.650
were having problems with?

00:41:57.650 --> 00:42:00.710
How can we improve the
training for the next round

00:42:00.710 --> 00:42:03.560
of deployment to make sure
that people don't have

00:42:03.560 --> 00:42:05.220
to call about those things?

00:42:05.220 --> 00:42:08.090
What are the active
bugs in the system that

00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:12.620
are spiking that maybe, just
hadn't been contemplated

00:42:12.620 --> 00:42:16.070
in the initial implementation
that we can, again,

00:42:16.070 --> 00:42:20.360
prepare to eradicate for in
the next round of deployment?

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:24.260
And so some of the things that
we found were delegate access.

00:42:24.260 --> 00:42:27.470
Everyone was having-- there
was a huge spike and people not

00:42:27.470 --> 00:42:31.790
being able to identify how to
delegate to other individuals

00:42:31.790 --> 00:42:34.070
to handle their T&amp;E.
They were having trouble

00:42:34.070 --> 00:42:39.860
uploading their expense receipts
where those were required.

00:42:39.860 --> 00:42:44.420
They were also, having
issues in the replication

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:46.920
of their old profile
to the new profile.

00:42:46.920 --> 00:42:48.140
So some of these--

00:42:48.140 --> 00:42:51.500
like delegate access--
is better FAQs,

00:42:51.500 --> 00:42:52.790
better information available.

00:42:52.790 --> 00:42:56.300
Better training for the next
rollout-- process improvement.

00:42:56.300 --> 00:42:58.970
Some of the others were issues
with the technology that

00:42:58.970 --> 00:43:01.400
either needed improvement
or had bugs in them that

00:43:01.400 --> 00:43:03.020
needed to be fixed.

00:43:03.020 --> 00:43:06.050
But we're able to
monitor them now in a way

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:07.970
that we know something
real is happening

00:43:07.970 --> 00:43:10.820
